Z||000000||Red2m4||11-30-2001||03:34 AM||||<BR>Was looking through a JC Whitney Catalog and noticed they sell a gauge that tells you how many MPG you're getting.  How does this work? Perhaps I should ask DOES it work?||||reg||1
Z||000001||SLYGUY||11-30-2001||03:43 AM||||I remember reading about those in JC Whitney catalogs. A mechanic friend of mine told me that it just simply is hooked up to your carbeurator/manifold vacuum pressure. The nicer you are to the gas pedal the less vacuum is created and the needle stays in the green $ zone. <P>Very Very simple mechanics. Not rocket science or anything. <P>Some people will make anything for a buck!<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||11-30-2001||06:05 AM||||i know on the 87/88 tbirds and probably some of the lincolns had digital dashes.<P>They had mpg output on the screen.  I think there was a sensor in the fueltank or the fuel pressure and it calculates it by the miles.<P>I have to check my tbirds service manual on what sensor it would run off.<P>------------------<BR>Fiero Technician<P>74 Vw Beetle<BR>83 Honda Nighthawk 550<BR>84 Pontiac Fiero<BR>87 Ford Thunderbird Turbo<P>Selling a car is like giving your own kid for adoption.||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||11-30-2001||06:26 AM||||It's a vacuum gauge.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||06:48 AM||||yep, just a vacuum gauge. the wider you open the throttle, the less the vacuum. your 'shift' lite in panel is same thing, just hooked up to a lite bulb.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||11-30-2001||09:43 AM||||Lincoln Continnentals back in the early 80's had a numerical digital display telling you how many miles per gallon you're getting.  If you floor it, you can make it display "0"mpg.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I <I>assume</I> it works off of a vacume.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||09:53 AM||||A couple of older 3-series BMW's(89-90) I was test driving a couple of years ago had those.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||12:39 PM||||60's mopar had those too, I had a couple of Cudas, they called it 'Economy Gauge'||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eriamjh||11-30-2001||01:25 PM||||I doubt the JC whitney gage tells you how many MPG you are getting, but who knows.<P>Th so called "economy gages" work on vacuum.  Basically, an accelerating car has a lower vacuum and hence a lower MPG.<P>Real MPG gages measure fuel flow and do a quick calculation based on the vehicle's speed.  It's very accurate, but is only the "instantaneous" mileage, not the average.  Others average the fuel use over a longer time and give a near average.<P>The decent ones will tell you how many miles till empty and can help you push your gas to the limit between fill ups.||||reg||1
Z||000009||soup||11-30-2001||03:43 PM||||MY Lebaron had that, it was digital though... Actually quite helpfull on long trips but just another thing to go wrong as far as im concerned||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mark Matthews||12-05-2001||01:25 AM||||My 86' Audi Turbo Quattro had a high-tek gauge that gave you multiple readings.  Average mpg, trip mpg, miles left till empty, and average speed.  Regular daily driving no matter what week it was, I would always reset it when filling up the tank.  It was always 20-22 mpg and 23-25 mph.  This was city and highway.  Those dang stop lights kill ya.  When I would first reset it, I could just watch the miles left drop as I just sat there.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||1
Z||000011||camon||12-05-2001||01:58 AM||||That gauge would have lasted all of about 1 week in my car til I would have forcefully removed it.<P>Sure your still using the same fuel with or without the gauge. Sure it would be nice to know how many miles you got left but just seeing it dropping would have drove me nuts!<P>Imagine explaining that one to the cop that nails you for running a red light...." but officer I had to run that light I was just sitting there wasting fuel...my gauge was telling me so.......by the way officer got go because sitting here talking to you is wasting fuel too"......<P>Nope they can keep those gauges!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||10
